  "strategy": "We are going to use a dynamical systems optimization approach combined with simulated annealing.\nThe key idea is to represent the sequence as a function in the frequency space and optimize the Time-Frequency distribution under constraints to reduce the peak autocorrelation.\n\nThis approach leverages the Fourier Transform property where convolution corresponds to multiplication in frequency space.\nSpecifically, we convert the sequence into its Fourier coefficients, ensuring they satisfy:\n1. Positivity in time-space\n2. Unit sum (normalized energy)\n3. Continuous function sampling\n\nSteps to Implement:\n1. If parent sequence exists, use it to initialize, otherwise generate modest variations of sine and cosine functions (oscillatory sequences) normalized to unit energy.\n2. Apply time-frequency transformation and constraint optimization using simulated annealing with physics-based temperature control and cooling schedule.\n3. For each energy-normalized candidate, evaluate its autocorrelation (peak) and compute the objective function (J(a)).\n4. Monitor the time budget and update the best candidate based on lower J values.\n5. If time allows, expand to longer sequences (resampling) and continue optimization.\n\nDual-normalization technique:\n- First maintain unit time-sum while optimizing the Fourier Transform for minimum peak autocorrelation.\n- Then convert normalized Fourier coefficients back to time-space while preserving the energy.\n\nThis method reduces the peak autocorrelation by manipulating time-frequency distribution, potentially leading to significantly lower J values (target 1.5) than the previous 1.506808.",
